+# Agent Note: Optional client session hydration and generation readiness
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-22-client-session-hydration-readiness.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+An embedding can render a useful session list and history before the Host transport reconnects, but the client Runtime must not own persistence, browser storage, retention, or product-specific cache policy. Cached state also cannot be mistaken for a live authoritative baseline.
+
+Reconnect replay creates a second lifecycle problem: pending questions and approvals are replayed before the connection callback. Clearing them during resync removes just-replayed requests and makes answerable UI disappear.
+
+## Decision
+
+The Runtime defines the optional `sessionHydration` capability seam. A provider may synchronously supply non-authoritative list and history-window seeds and receive successful authoritative commits. Reads and commits are fail-soft: adapter failures never interrupt the live Runtime. No storage implementation or key is part of this package.
+
+The Runtime provides the replayable `sessionBaselineReadiness` snapshot store. Every connection receives a monotonically increasing generation and starts `pending`. It becomes `ready` only when that generation establishes its own live list baseline and the selected Session's live history baseline; any required failure publishes `error`. An older successful generation cannot make a failed reconnect ready.
+
+On disconnect, pending interaction identities are marked stale but remain visible. Mux replay removes confirmed identities from that stale set before `handleConnected`; only unconfirmed stale identities are pruned. This matches the transport ordering without giving cached data authority.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Embeddings choose persistence and retention policy outside the Runtime.
+- Official behavior is unchanged when no provider exists.
+- Cached list/history can paint immediately but never satisfy live readiness.
+- Adapter exceptions degrade to normal live loading.
+- Pending questions survive replay-before-resync and disappear only when the new generation does not confirm them.
+- The capability is revision-sensitive until adopted by an official release; consumers must gate it against an exact verified Runtime.
